-- THE WOOD IS DRAWN SLAB BY SLAB (rome, 2026-08-03: "this is much better but
-- the woods looks a bit fucked").
--
-- The rest of the sheet came right in mig 168; the wood did not, and it was the
-- last thing laid out as ONE lump of 170 rooms. Laid that way it embeds at
-- 226/410 arrows wrong, because its eight maze cores were wired together for
-- connectivity rather than geometry and their loops do not close.
--
-- But the wood was AUTHORED IN FOUR SLABS, and inside a slab it very nearly
-- works — measured, slab by slab: 6/76, 4/86, 12/128, 40/132. Sixty-two wrong
-- out of four hundred and twenty-two, against two hundred and twenty-six for
-- the same rooms in one piece. The contradictions are almost all in the doors
-- BETWEEN slabs, which is exactly where a human drew a line and started a new
-- file.
--
-- So the wood is laid out the way it was written: each slab embedded on its own
-- grid, then hung off its own seams, and always growing WEST AND SOUTH — away
-- from the fortress, never back across the road. Everything that is not wood
-- keeps precisely the square mig 168 gave it.
--
--   the wood   226/410 -> 94/410 arrows wrong
--   the road   4/136 (untouched)   the dens 0/154 (untouched)
--   overlapping squares: 0
--
-- What is left is the wood's own defect and no layout can reach it: 94 doors
-- that disagree about where they lead. Fixing those means re-cutting the ways
-- between the cores, which changes the maze people walk — rome's call, not a
-- map job.
